WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:08.610
Hi! Why don't fridge magnets fall off? Today we'll learn about the properties of magnets.

2
00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:16.098
Magnets attract things made of iron, steel, nickel and cobalt. These are magnetic materials.

3
00:00:16.248 --> 00:00:23.562
Plastic, wood and paper are not attracted. Copper and aluminium are metals, but they aren't attracted either.

4
00:00:23.712 --> 00:00:30.546
Magnetic force can pass through paper and plastic, so a magnet can hold an iron clip through paper.

5
00:00:30.696 --> 00:00:36.810
A magnet is strongest at its ends. These are called the poles.

6
00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:41.802
A magnet has two poles: north, N, and south, S.

7
00:00:41.952 --> 00:00:48.186
Two north poles push each other away, and so do two south poles. Like poles repel.

8
00:00:48.336 --> 00:00:53.586
A north pole and a south pole pull together. Unlike poles attract.

9
00:00:53.736 --> 00:01:01.890
Hang a magnet so it can turn freely, and it stops pointing north and south. That's how a compass works.

10
00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:08.154
Watch out! Many pupils think magnets attract all metals. That's wrong!

11
00:01:08.304 --> 00:01:12.882
A magnet can't pick up an aluminium drink can or a copper key.

12
00:01:13.032 --> 00:01:20.202
Only magnetic materials such as iron, steel, nickel and cobalt are attracted.

13
00:01:20.352 --> 00:01:28.602
Your turn! Can a magnet pick up an iron nail? Pause and think.

14
00:01:28.752 --> 00:01:35.322
Yes. Iron is a magnetic material. Did you get it right?

15
00:01:35.472 --> 00:01:44.610
Another one! Can a magnet pick up an aluminium drink can? Pause and think.

16
00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:51.858
No. Aluminium isn't a magnetic material. Did you get it right?

17
00:01:52.008 --> 00:02:01.866
Here's another! What happens when the south poles of two magnets come close? Pause and think.

18
00:02:02.016 --> 00:02:07.914
Like poles repel, so they push apart. Did you get it right?

19
00:02:08.064 --> 00:02:16.698
Try one more! Where is a magnet's force strongest? Pause and think.

20
00:02:16.848 --> 00:02:22.170
At the poles, its two ends. Did you get it right?

21
00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:33.306
Try one more! A magnet hangs and turns freely. Which way does it point when it stops? Pause and think.

22
00:02:33.456 --> 00:02:38.682
It points north and south. Did you get it right?

23
00:02:38.832 --> 00:02:49.386
Last one! What happens when the N pole of one magnet comes near the S pole of another? Pause and think.

24
00:02:49.536 --> 00:02:55.650
Unlike poles attract, so they pull together. Did you get it right?

25
00:02:55.800 --> 00:03:07.890
Remember: magnets attract iron, steel, nickel and cobalt. They have north and south poles, and are strongest at the poles. Like poles repel, unlike poles attract.

26
00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:13.194
Use a magnet to test which things at home it attracts!

